WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.823
Similar right triangles preserve ratios of corresponding sides, even when their sizes change

00:00:05.823 --> 00:00:11.245
Fix an acute angle and compare the side opposite it with the hypotenuse

00:00:11.245 --> 00:00:15.833
That consistent ratio is called the sine of the chosen angle.

00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:23.578
Suppose the original triangle has opposite side three, adjacent side four, and hypotenuse five

00:00:23.578 --> 00:00:28.182
Dividing every length by five normalizes the hypotenuse to one

00:00:28.182 --> 00:00:34.167
Its vertical and horizontal legs then have lengths three fifths and four fifths.

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:38.825
Enlarge the triangle by a factor of two

00:00:38.825 --> 00:00:44.703
The opposite side becomes six and the hypotenuse ten, leaving the same sine ratio

00:00:44.703 --> 00:00:51.000
This is why an angle determines trigonometric ratios independently of the scale of the triangle.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.850
To recover a side, multiply the hypotenuse by the known ratio

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.332
Ten times three fifths gives six

00:00:59.332 --> 00:01:08.433
First check that the triangle is right and identify sides relative to the marked angle; the hypotenuse always faces the right angle.
